WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.920
This is Kytos, an open lab notebook for biology's hardest predictions.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.500
Every run, failures included.

3
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.880
We took four supernodes from a KRAS contact core we had recorded earlier,

4
00:00:12.359 --> 00:00:16.140
the P-loop source, two intermediate nodes, and the known pocket.

5
00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:19.250
Then one control, delete a single edge.

6
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:27.260
On IonQ's Forte 1 noise model simulator at two thousand and forty-eight shots,

7
00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.560
the recorded graph lands on the pocket nineteen percent of the time.

8
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:35.500
With one edge removed, forty percent, both sit within a point of the exact answer.

9
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:44.200
A classical random walk on the same graph reaches the pocket only six to eight percent of the time.

10
00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.780
A different dynamic, not a better prediction.

11
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.120
Here's what this is not.

12
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.660
We picked the slice and the time knowing where the pocket was.

13
00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.820
It's a simulator, not hardware, and we claim no quantum advantage.

14
00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.320
The circuit runs on Qollab.

15
00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:00.760
Every number links to its source.

16
00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.500
This is Kytos.
